steps.body:
Every Java program lives in a class. Here the first class is always `public class Main`, and the program starts running at its `main` method. Java looks for the exact header `public static void main(String[] args)`, so copy it as is for now. Each statement ends with a semicolon `;`, and Java is case sensitive, so `system.out` will not compile.

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // A comment: Java ignores everything after //
        System.out.println("Hello");
        System.out.print("Same ");
        System.out.println("line");
        System.out.println();
    }
}
```

- `System.out.println("text")` prints the text, then ends the line.
- `System.out.print("text")` prints the text and stays on the same line, so the next output continues right after it.
- `System.out.println()` with nothing inside prints an empty line.

Text goes in double quotes. Single quotes are only for one character, so `'Hi'` is a compile error.

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // A three-line banner
        System.out.println("Welcome to Hack University");
        System.out.println("Learn Java one step at a time");
        System.out.println("Let's write some code!");
        System.out.println();
        // print does not end the line, so println finishes it
        System.out.print("Lesson 1: ");
        System.out.println("print");
    }
}

